"""Instructor talk tracks for Day 4. One entry per slide number: (say, do).
say = what to say, in plain words (list of paragraphs). do = an action or a question for the room (optional, may be "").
Key-terms slides are generated from the deck itself, so only the intro line is written here.
Only facts that are printed on the slides are quoted. Model wording is never promised.
"""

# ───────────────────────────── CLASS 7 ─────────────────────────────
C7 = {
    1: (["Welcome back. Yesterday Neo used three tools that we wired in by hand. Today we meet two ideas.",
         "First, the <b>standard plug</b> that connects an agent to any system. Second, the <b>guardrails</b> that keep it safe.",
         "By lunch you will have connected an agent, watched it use tools, and made it stop and ask a person."],
        "Before class: kernel selected, CELL S run, and make_mcp_config.py run once for the live demos. Mention there are 19 key terms and nobody needs to memorise them."),
    2: (["Quick recap of yesterday, in thirty seconds. An agent has four parts: brain, tools, memory and instructions. It works in a loop: think, act, observe, repeat.",
         "And one rule: the AI decides, the system executes, and only with tools we allow.",
         "So Neo can act. But look at the last line: those three tools were hand-built for one agent. A real company has dozens of systems."],
        "Ask: \"Who can name the four parts?\" Take one answer and move on."),
    3: (["Let me show you why that does not scale. Say we have 4 agents and 5 systems. If every agent needs its own connection to every system, that is 20 custom connections.",
         "With one standard plug, each agent plugs in once and each system plugs in once. That is 4 plus 5, which is 9.",
         "SAP people already know this idea. IDoc, OData and RFC are standard shapes, so a new system does not need a custom cable."],
        "Draw the grid on the board if you can. Read the question at the bottom aloud: \"If every new system needs its own custom connection, it does not scale.\""),
    4: (["Neo is the same character. What changes is how Neo connects to systems and how we keep that safe.",
         "Four things to watch for today: one standard plug, tools that are discovered and not typed, guardrails around every change, and a person who approves changes."],
        ""),
    5: (["This is the one idea for the morning: <b>one standard plug, a few simple guardrails.</b> That is MCP.",
         "By the end of the class you will connect an agent, watch it use tools, and make it stop and ask."],
        "Pause on this slide for a few seconds. Let it land."),
    6: (["Here is the plan for two hours. Part A is the problem and the plug. Part B is what a server offers. Part C is the agent working over MCP, with our first live demo.",
         "Part D is guardrails, with the second live demo. Part E is your turn: forty minutes of hands-on with five business questions."],
        "Point at the timings. Tell them Part E is the longest on purpose."),
    7: (["Same set-up as yesterday, with one addition. The agent still uses the chat model on this laptop. This time the tools sit behind a small program called an MCP server.",
         "It is one Python file I prepared. It reads two files, orders and vendors. Everything is mock data for our fictional company, Nova Industries."],
        "Run CELL S together. Read the ticks aloud. If a tick is missing, use the Fix line that is printed under it."),
    8: (["Part A: the plug. The idea in four words is \"USB-C for AI\".", "One shape that fits any device. We will connect, then discover."], ""),
    10: (["Start with what you already know. SAP does not build a custom cable to every other system. It uses standard interfaces: IDoc, OData, RFC. A new system plugs into a known shape.",
          "Agents had exactly the same problem. A custom connection for every system. MCP is the standard shape for AI: one plug, any system."],
         "Use the SAP comparison first. People already trust standard interfaces."),
    11: (["Read this picture left to right. On the left, AI apps. We call them MCP clients. Our notebook is one. Antigravity is one. VS Code is one.",
          "In the middle, the standard plug. On the right, the systems, which we call MCP servers. Our mock SAP orders is one server.",
          "Any client can use any server that speaks the standard. Nobody builds the pair twice."],
         "Trace the arrows with your finger or mouse."),
    12: (["This is Cell 1. We connect, then we ask the server what it offers. The server tells us its name, its protocol version, and that it offers tools, resources and prompts.",
          "Then we ask for the list of tools. Six names come back. Nobody typed those six names in the notebook. The server told us. That is called <b>discovery</b>."],
         "Run CELL 1. Ask: \"Where did these six names come from?\" Answer: from the server."),
    13: (["Compare yesterday and today. In Class 6 we typed the tool menu into the notebook. Today we ask the server for it.",
          "If we add a tool, yesterday we edited the notebook. Today we edit the server only, and any client can use it.",
          "And every tool now says whether it changes data. We will use that label in Part D."],
         "Contrast Cell 3 of Class 6 with Cell 5 of today."),
    14: (["Here is how discovery works, in three steps. The client asks: what do you offer? The server answers with, for each tool, a name, a description, its inputs and a read-only label. Then the agent's instructions list exactly these tools.",
          "One practical point: the descriptions matter. The model reads them to choose a tool. A vague description leads to a wrong choice."],
         "Run CELL 1 again if anyone missed the output."),
    15: (["Part B: three things. What can a system actually give to an agent? Tools, resources and prompts."], ""),
    18: (["A server offers three things. <b>Tools</b> are actions the AI may request, like check a status or create a ticket. <b>Resources</b> are data to read, like order lists, reports and policy text. <b>Prompts</b> are ready-made requests with blanks to fill.",
          "Read, do, or ask in a saved way. Three shapes, one standard."],
         "Say the three words and give one example of each from the order system."),
    19: (["Now the SAP translation. A tool is a BAPI or function module. A resource is a report or a table view. A prompt is a transaction variant, a saved way of asking.",
          "A server is like an OData service: it offers the three things. A client is whatever uses them."],
         "Go row by row. Each row links an MCP word to something they know."),
    20: (["This is Cell 2. We list every tool with a label. Five say read-only. One, create_ticket, says it changes data.",
          "Only one tool in this whole server can change anything, and it is clearly marked. Remember that amber label. Part D depends on it."],
         "Run CELL 2. Point at the one amber label."),
    21: (["Same cell, second point. Ask for a real order and you get its details. Ask for an order that does not exist and you do not get a crash. You get a readable message.",
          "That matters for agents: a readable error lets the agent try again or tell you what is missing."],
         "Read the two outputs aloud."),
    22: (["A quick quiz before I reveal the middle column. Is \"check the status of a PO\" a tool, a resource or a prompt?",
          "Create a follow-up ticket is a tool, not a resource, because it does something. A resource is only read."],
         "Cover the middle column. Ask the room for each row, then reveal."),
    23: (["Cell 3, resources. Each resource has an address, like orders://summary. The client reads it. It is not run, only read.",
          "This summary shows twelve orders and how they split by status and by plant. That is the data behind our demos."],
         "Run CELL 3."),
    24: (["Prompts. The server offers saved requests with blanks. Here the blank is the plant, and we give it plant 1000.",
          "The server fills in the blank and hands back the finished request text. We will use this prompt in the live demo."],
         "Run CELL 3 (prompts part)."),
    25: (["Now the whole protocol for one call. On the left the request: call the tool check_approval with a value of twelve lakh. On the right the result: who must approve.",
          "No magic. A plain-text message goes out, a plain-text message comes back. The id on your screen will differ from mine."],
         "Run CELL 4. Tell them this is for understanding only; they will never write this by hand."),
    26: (["Part C: the agent over MCP. Same loop as yesterday, new plug. The menu now comes from the server."], ""),
    28: (["This is the loop from Class 6 with one new actor. The AI writes a request. The program checks the gate. The server runs the function. The AI reads the result.",
          "The new part is step 2, the gate. It is plain code between what the AI asked and what actually runs. Cell 6 runs this."],
         "Compare with the four steps from yesterday."),
    29: (["Cell 5. Look at the instructions the agent gets. They have the same structure as Class 6, but nobody typed the tool list. It was built from the server's answer."],
         "Run CELL 5. Read two of the tool descriptions aloud."),
    30: (["Cell 6. Our question is the one from the live demo: which orders for Plant 1000 are blocked and why?",
          "The agent thinks, then acts by calling list_orders for plant 1000 with status Blocked. It observes four orders, and answers.",
          "Look at the block reasons: credit check, price variance, missing goods receipt, credit check. The facts came from the server, not from the model's memory."],
         "Run CELL 6. If the model words it differently, say so: the tools are exact, the wording is not."),
    31: (["Now the same question in Antigravity. I want you to see that a real agent tool uses the same plug.",
          "I will open the Day 4 folder. Antigravity reads the config that our setup script made. I check the server is connected, then I ask the question.",
          "Watch for four things and I will say them out loud: it lists the tools, it chooses list_orders, it receives facts, and it writes a table."],
         "Live demo, about 10 minutes. Follow antigravity/TASK.md. Use the names you actually see on screen. Keep a backup recording."),
    32: (["Here is the answer key. Four orders. We gave Antigravity only the question. It chose the tool, filled in the arguments and wrote the table.",
          "Compare what you saw with this slide."],
         "If the agent differs, treat it as a discussion point and not an error."),
    33: (["Now my favourite moment. Yesterday, to add a tool, we edited the notebook. Today I restart the server with one new tool and change nothing in the agent.",
          "Tools before: six. Tools after: seven. The new one is vendor_scorecard. The agent just sees a bigger menu. That is <b>plug and play</b>."],
         "Run CELL 7."),
    34: (["And the new tool answers. A scorecard per vendor: rating, orders, delayed, blocked and total value.",
          "Notice who does the adding: the tool. The agent only chooses to use it. That is the right split."],
         "Ask: \"Why do we want the tool to do the adding and not the model?\""),
    35: (["Part D: guardrails. Limits that live in code, not in hope."], ""),
    38: (["Back to SAP. A PO above a limit waits for release. Users get display or change authorisation, never everything.",
          "An agent that can act needs the same thing: limits that the program enforces, not limits we ask the model to remember."],
         "Guardrails are the authorisation concept for agents."),
    39: (["Four guardrails. One: read-only first. Two: an allow-list, so the agent only gets the tools it needs. Three: a step limit, so there are no endless loops. Four: human approval before any change.",
          "SAP comparison: display authorisation, a role, a job timeout."],
         "Name the four, then give the SAP comparison."),
    40: (["Where do guardrails sit? Between the AI and the system. The AI asks, which is only text. The program checks: is it read-only, is it on the menu, are we under the step limit, was it approved? Then the server runs only what passed.",
          "Each guardrail is a few lines of ordinary code. The AI cannot talk its way past them."],
         "This is the gate from Part C, now with its four checks."),
    41: (["Cell 8, guardrails one to three. First, read-only: asking for create_ticket is refused with a clear message. Second, an allow-list of two tools: the menu shrinks to two, and asking for list_orders is refused.",
          "Third, a step limit of one: the loop stops with \"Stopped: step limit reached\". Notice that errors go back to the agent as observations, so it can recover or tell you what is missing. The first two lines are exact; the Think and Act lines above the third will differ by model."],
         "Run CELL 8."),
    42: (["Cell 9, human approval. Only one function changed: the gate. If the tool is read-only, let it through. If not, ask a person.",
          "I ask for a ticket on PO 4500012345 about the credit check. The program stops and shows me the tool and its arguments. I type yes, and the ticket is created. I run it again, type no, and nothing is created."],
         "Run CELL 9 twice: yes once, no once. The loop itself is untouched."),
    43: (["This is a release strategy for AI. In SAP a PO above the limit waits for release. For the agent, a tool that changes data waits for a person.",
          "The approver sees the PO first. Here the person sees the tool and its arguments. Not released means nothing is posted. Declined means nothing is changed."],
         "The SAP people already believe in this. Say so."),
    44: (["Now the guardrail demo in a real tool. In Antigravity, I turn on the mode that asks before running commands or tools. I enable the write server. Then I ask for a ticket on PO 4500012345.",
          "Watch: the agent <b>pauses</b>. It shows the call and its arguments. Nothing has changed yet. That pause is the guardrail.",
          "I approve once and we see the ticket file grow. I run it again, decline, and the file does not grow."],
         "Live demo, about 10 minutes. Steps are in antigravity/TASK.md. Contrast: with the read-only server enabled, the server itself refuses. Two layers: the server can refuse and the client can ask."),
    45: (["Not every job needs an agent. If the rules are fixed, use normal automation: an approval limit lookup, the Tuesday and Friday payment run, posting an invoice that passed the three-way match.",
          "Use an agent when the next step depends on what you find: a PO delay investigation, IDoc error triage, invoice exceptions."],
         "Ask the room for one process of each kind from their own projects."),
    46: (["Cell 10 makes the point with numbers. The approval lookup done by plain automation uses zero model calls. The agent can do the same job, but it typically needs two model calls and two steps. Same answer, slower, and it can slip. The notebook prints your real call counts on the right.",
          "If you can write the rule, write the rule."],
         "Run CELL 10."),
    47: (["A short overview: multi-agent. Imagine a lead agent that splits the job and hands each part to a specialist: procurement, finance, logistics. Each specialist is one of the agents we already built, with its own tools.",
          "That is all multi-agent is: several of what we built, plus a coordinator. We will not build one today."],
         "Overview only. Keep it to two minutes."),
    48: (["Part E: your turn. You will ask five questions through the agent, and for each one you note two things: which tool it chose, and whether it needed approval."], "Forty minutes. Cells 11 and 12."),
    50: (["Four tasks. Ask five business questions in Cell 11. Note the tool it chose. Note whether it needed approval. Then score it with Cell 12, where four questions are checked by code.",
          "Change only the lines marked \"change me\"."],
         "Cell 9 must have been run first, so the write server and the approval gate are active. Work in pairs or teams."),
    51: (["Here are the five questions and what we expect. Plant 1000 blocked orders, the vendor contact for a PO, who must approve a PO, and so on.",
          "If the agent picks a different tool order, that is a discussion point, not an error. Compare the answers with the expected column."],
         "Run CELL 11. The table prints the tools chosen, the steps and whether approval was needed."),
    52: (["Fill this worksheet in while the notebook runs. Write the tools in the order Cell 11 prints them. Then compare with your neighbour."], "Hand out or share on screen."),
    53: (["Cell 12 is the scoreboard. Each row says which tool must be used and what the answer must contain. Question 4 must trigger an approval and produce a ticket id.",
          "If the approver types no, that row shows a cross by design, because nothing was created. That is the guardrail working."],
         "Run CELL 12. Fast finishers can add their own rows."),
    54: (["Here is the list from Class 6, now with a working mechanism behind it. An agent may do alone: look up a status, find a contact, check a limit, summarise delays.",
          "A person approves first: creating a ticket, posting documents, releasing payments, changing master data especially bank details, and emailing vendors or customers."],
         "Ask the room to add one more item to each column."),
    55: (["Quick myth check. MCP is not a new SAP product; it is an open standard on top of interfaces you already have. Being on MCP does not make something safe; the guardrails do. And the AI does not run the tool: the AI asks, the program checks, the server runs."],
         "Use as a quick correction list."),
    56: (["This page is your handout. MCP is the plug: a server offers tools, resources and prompts, the client discovers them, and adding a tool to the server serves every agent. Guardrails: read-only first, allow-list, step limit, human approval. And not every job needs an agent."],
         "Share or print this slide."),
    57: (["Quick quiz. I ask, you answer aloud, then I reveal."], "Ask each question aloud before revealing the answer."),
    58: (["Three things to remember. One: MCP is USB-C for AI. Two: guardrails keep it safe. Three: not every job needs an agent."], ""),
    59: (["We have now seen every piece. After lunch the question is: how do they fit together into one real solution, and where do we start?"],
         "Bridge to the afternoon. Mention the afternoon needs Ollama running again."),
}

# ───────────────────────────── CLASS 8 ─────────────────────────────
C8 = {
    1: (["Welcome back. This morning we met MCP and guardrails. We now have every piece: prompts, knowledge, agents, tools and guardrails.",
         "This afternoon we assemble them, check the result, and learn where to start. At the end each team presents."],
        "Before class: CELL S run. Check that NotebookLM and Antigravity are approved for mock data, or use the notebook alternatives."),
    2: (["Quick recap of the morning. MCP is USB-C for AI: tools, resources, prompts, one standard plug. Guardrails: read-only first, limits, and a person approves changes. And fixed rules belong to normal automation.",
         "Neo can plug into systems safely. But each piece so far was a separate demo."],
        "Thirty seconds. Ask: \"What is the one guardrail you would never skip?\""),
    3: (["Imagine a sponsor asks you this: can we have one assistant for the SAP operations team? It should answer from our policies and from live SAP data, and never change anything on its own.",
         "That is not one demo. It needs several pieces working together, and a way to trust the result."],
        "Set the scene: a real request needs all four days."),
    4: (["Neo is the same character, now with everything: prompt and rules from Classes 3 and 4, knowledge from documents from Class 5, an agent that decides the steps from Class 6, and tools through MCP from Class 7."], ""),
    5: (["The one idea for the afternoon: <b>start simple, and add only what the problem needs.</b> A prompt first, then knowledge, then an agent.",
         "That order saves money, time and risk."],
        "Pause here."),
    6: (["The plan for two hours. Part 1: start simple. Part 2: assemble the parts, which is the core. Part 3: business concerns, can we trust it, afford it and keep it private. Part 4: the capstone, where you build, test and pitch.",
         "Part 4 is forty minutes of team work, then five-minute pitches."],
        "Point at the timings."),
    7: (["Same set-up as this morning, plus the documents. A chat model, an embedding model for the documents, and the MCP server. Nothing leaves this laptop."],
        "Run CELL S. Read the ticks. The same server file as Class 7 is started with writes enabled, but a person-approval gate protects it later."),
    8: (["Part 1: start simple. Which approach does the job? The simplest one that works."], ""),
    10: (["In SAP you do not write a custom ABAP program when a standard report answers the question. You pick the simplest tool that works.",
          "Do the same with AI. A prompt if the text is in front of you. RAG if the answer is in documents. An agent only for multi-step work with systems."],
         "Set the rule before any demo."),
    11: (["Here is the table to remember. Summarise, draft or classify text: a good prompt. Answer from company documents: RAG. Multi-step task using systems: an agent.",
          "Look at the examples. One-line summary of a supplier email is a prompt. \"Who approves a PO above 10 lakh?\" is RAG. \"Why is PO 4500012348 blocked and who is the contact?\" is an agent."],
         "Walk the table row by row. Ask the room for another example per row."),
    12: (["Cell 1: prompt only. We give the model a supplier email. The truck was held up by rain, and delivery moves from Friday to Monday.",
          "We ask for a one-sentence summary. No documents, no tools. It is the cheapest and fastest approach, and for this job it is enough."],
         "Run CELL 1. Remind them the wording on screen is a target; their model will word it differently."),
    13: (["Cell 2 asks: can the model choose the approach? We give it five needs and see whether it says prompt, RAG or agent.",
          "If you see a warning mark, that is the lesson. Choosing the approach is itself a decision that you should test. In a real project a person usually makes this choice once, per use case."],
         "Run CELL 2."),
    14: (["Here are five SAP use cases we will use for the capstone: invoice exception, PO delay analysis, IDoc error triage, vendor query and month-end close support.",
          "The last column says where the answer comes from: MCP tools, RAG documents, or both. Think about which one your team would like."],
         "These are the five use cases in Cell 10."),
    15: (["Part 2: assemble. From four pieces to one: knowledge, tools, rules and an agent."], ""),
    17: (["This is the whole afternoon on one slide. Read it top to bottom. The user asks. The prompt and rules shape it. The agent decides the steps. It can use company knowledge through RAG and system tools through MCP. The LLM writes the answer, and a checked, structured response goes back to the user.",
          "Every row is a class you already did. Cells 3 to 7 build it, in this order."],
         "Point at the class number on each row."),
    18: (["Which tool for which part? The brain is Ollama. Knowledge is NotebookLM, or the notebook. Prompts are written in Ollama or VS Code. Agent and tools are Antigravity with MCP.",
          "A warning: NotebookLM and Antigravity are cloud tools. Get approval first and use mock data only. The notebook cells give the same result without them."],
         "Repeat the data warning clearly."),
    19: (["Cell 3: knowledge as a function. Same four steps as Class 5: split, embed, store, search. The new idea is that the search is a function that returns clauses with their ids.",
          "That means it can sit on a tool menu next to the MCP tools. The notebook shows how many chunks were stored, from four documents."],
         "Run CELL 3."),
    20: (["Here is what one chunk looks like. It carries an id, Procurement Policy section 6.2, and the text: a price variance above 1 percent blocks the invoice for review.",
          "The id becomes the citation. And look at the size: the answer needs only 22 words, not all four documents. This is the clause the capstone question needs."],
         "Say the id aloud so they recognise it later."),
    21: (["Cell 4: one menu, two sources. Six tools come from the MCP server and one comes from our own knowledge function. To the agent, both are just tools.",
          "That is the key idea. RAG and MCP are not rivals. They are two items on the same menu."],
         "Run CELL 4."),
    22: (["Cell 5: the assistant's identity is just data. A role, a list of rules and an answer format.",
          "Rules like: use only facts returned by your tools, cite the clause id, you may only read, and if something is missing say what is missing. Edit a rule, run it again, and the behaviour changes. This is Classes 3 and 4 in one place."],
         "Run CELL 5. Show one change live."),
    23: (["Cell 6 is the moment everything comes together. One question: PO 4500012348 is blocked. Why, who must approve it, what does our policy say about price variance, and who is the vendor contact?",
          "The agent asks for the order, then the approver, then the policy, then the vendor. Four tools. Nobody wrote this sequence."],
         "Run CELL 6. The rows marked exact are computed from the data; the policy row is a target."),
    24: (["Here is who provided each fact. The prompt gave the role, rules and format. The MCP tools gave the order, the approver and the vendor, live. RAG gave the policy clause, with its id.",
          "The LLM writes. It does not know. Every fact has a source we can point to, and that is why we can check the answer in Part 3."],
         "This is the sentence to remember."),
    25: (["Part 3: business concerns. A demo is not a solution. A sponsor will ask about accuracy, cost, speed, privacy and oversight."], ""),
    28: (["Five questions every sponsor asks, and the cell that answers each. Accuracy: code checks every number and citation. Cost: we count model calls and tokens. Speed: we time each approach. Privacy: a local model, only three chunks sent. Oversight: a person approves changes.",
          "If you can answer these five, your pilot is credible."],
         "Each row is answered by a cell in this part."),
    29: (["Cell 7, a structured response. Not just free text, but fields: the answer, the tools used, the steps, whether it was checked, any problems, and whether a human is needed.",
          "An application can act on that. It is like sending an IDoc instead of a free-text email."],
         "Run CELL 7."),
    30: (["Now the check. The check is plain code. Every number and citation in the answer must appear in the tool results.",
          "On the left, a grounded answer passes. On the right, a made-up answer is caught and flagged. A flagged answer sets needs human."],
         "Run CELL 7 (check part)."),
    31: (["Cell 8: cost and speed. Prompt only is one call. RAG is one call plus an embedding, with more tokens because three clauses are added. The agent needs five calls for our capstone question, four tools and the answer, and the whole conversation is re-sent at each step.",
          "So more steps means more tokens and more waiting. That is why we start simple."],
         "Run CELL 8. Your real numbers depend on your model and laptop; use the ones on your screen."),
    32: (["Cell 9: privacy and oversight. The model runs locally, so nothing leaves this laptop. RAG sends only three of the 39 chunks. Cloud tools get mock data only, after approval.",
          "For oversight, reading runs alone and changing waits for a person. The same approval gate from this morning is used here."],
         "Run CELL 9. It triggers an approval prompt for a ticket request."),
    33: (["Before I show you the list, tell me: what would you require before a pilot, and before this touches live data?",
          "Here is mine: read-only tools first, answers checked in code, a step limit, a test plan that re-runs. Then IT and security approval, a person approving every change, no real vendor or bank data in cloud tools, and an owner for the document library."],
         "Collect the room's list first. They reuse it in Cell 12."),
    34: (["Part 4: the capstone. Pick a process, build it, test it, pitch it. You have forty minutes."], "Teams of 3 or 4. Cells 10 to 12."),
    36: (["Your capstone has four parts. Brain: Ollama, the local model. Knowledge: NotebookLM, or the documents in the notebook. Prompts: write the assistant's role, rules and output format. Agent and tools: Antigravity with MCP, or the notebook agent.",
          "Cloud tools only if approved, and mock data only."],
         "Teams of 3 or 4."),
    37: (["Four tasks, forty minutes. Pick one SAP process in Cell 10. Ask a multi-step question and check the tools, the answer and the response. Test it in Cell 11. Fill in your pitch card in Cell 12.",
          "Change only the lines marked \"change me\"."],
         "Start the timer."),
    38: (["Cell 10: five processes to choose from. Try more than one. As you try, ask yourselves: which need only documents, which only MCP, which both?"],
         "Run CELL 10. Walk around and help teams choose."),
    39: (["Cell 11 is your test plan. Three kinds of tests, as in Class 5: direct questions, reworded questions, and questions with no answer. Each is scored by code.",
          "A cross is a good lesson. It tells you where to look: wrong tool, wrong clause, or the model ignored the result."],
         "Run CELL 11."),
    40: (["Reading a cross. Wrong tool: the agent chose a tool that cannot answer; fix the tool descriptions. Wrong clause: retrieval did not return the right id; fix the chunking or the embedding model. Ignored the result: the facts were there; fix the rules or use a stronger model.",
          "The printed tools tell you which one it is."],
         "Celebrate a wrong answer: it shows you where to look."),
    41: (["Cell 12 prints your pitch card from what you built. Four things to say: the process, the prompt you used, a live demo with the tools it called, and the guardrails you would require.",
          "Five minutes each."],
         "Run CELL 12. The card fills itself."),
    42: (["When it is your turn: show one SAP process, the prompt, and a live demo with the tools called. Then say the guardrails you would require and where you would start on Monday.",
          "While others present, judge with one question: would I trust this answer?"],
         "Five minutes per team. Ask the room to listen for the guardrails."),
    43: (["Quick myth check. More parts do not mean a better solution; start simple. The agent is not always right; it can pick the wrong tool, so check in code. RAG, MCP and agents are not rivals; they are parts of one assistant. And a demo is not a product."],
         "Use as a quick correction list."),
    44: (["This is the page to keep. The parts: prompt and rules, RAG for documents, MCP for live data, an agent to decide the steps, an answer check. Where to start: a good prompt, then documents, then an agent with tools, only when the problem needs it.",
          "And the five business concerns."],
         "Share or print this slide."),
    45: (["How to keep learning with the same free tools. Step 1: prompts, with Ollama and VS Code; save your best role, rules and format. Step 2: knowledge, RAG on approved documents. Step 3: tools, an MCP server over a read-only export. Step 4: a pilot with guardrails, tests and IT approval.",
          "Each step reuses something from this course. The roadmap file has the detail."],
         "Point to ROADMAP.md."),
    46: (["Final quiz. When is a prompt enough? When do you add RAG? When do you add an agent? And how do you check an answer in code?"], "Ask each aloud before revealing."),
    47: (["Three things. One: start simple. Two: one assistant made of known parts. Three: the business concerns decide: accuracy, cost, speed, privacy and oversight."], ""),
    48: (["That is the end of the course. You can now build it, test it and explain it.",
          "Take one SAP process back to your team. Start with a prompt. Add only what it needs. Keep a person in the loop. Thank you."],
         "Invite questions. Point to ROADMAP.md."),
}

